From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: "D.R.racer" <drracer@seznam.cz>
Date: Sun, 25 Oct 2026 15:39:47 +0100
Subject: [PATCH] media: cedrus: add a JPEG encoder for the A10/A20 VE

The A10/A20 video engine encodes baseline JPEG on its H.264 encoder block
in JPEG mode. Add it as a fourth video node, cedrus-jpeg-enc, with the
stateful encoder API of dev-encoder.rst, as a still-image encoder like
the other JPEG encoders in the tree (no frame rate).

- OUTPUT takes linear NV12 in full-range BT.601 (JFIF; the VE does not
  convert). The format is rounded up to whole macroblocks: the ISP reads
  whole macroblocks, and a height ending inside one would read its last
  rows from the chroma plane. The image size is the OUTPUT crop at the
  top-left corner, any size within the last macroblock; the client fills
  the padding.
- CAPTURE gives one baseline JFIF image (4:2:0) per buffer, MMAP or
  DMABUF. The kernel writes the headers (standard Huffman tables, which
  the VE has built in) and EOI; V4L2_CID_JPEG_COMPRESSION_QUALITY (1-100,
  default 75) scales the standard quantisation tables as libjpeg does.
- A frame that does not fit the CAPTURE buffer completes with
  V4L2_BUF_FLAG_ERROR and resets the VE; the next frame is unaffected.

What the hardware needs, found by testing:
- The bit writer takes its position from VLE_OFFSET when the stream
  addresses are written, so the offset goes first; written afterwards,
  the scan overwrites the header. The VE also mixes up a partial last
  header word, so the header is padded to a multiple of 4 bytes with
  0xff fill bytes before SOS.
- The DC level shift is an integer bias, 0x400 / Qdc, subtracted after
  quantisation: exact only if Qdc divides 1024. Both DC quantisers are
  rounded down to a power of two (the DQT carries the value used).
- The quantiser multiplies by a 16-bit reciprocal; for Q = 1 (0xffff)
  its rounding term must be 1, or every level comes out one too low.
- The quantiser adds 0.5 to the rounding term it is given, so even
  quantisers round 1/(2Q) early. This only matters for Q <= 8 (quality
  above ~85), where PSNR stays within 0.2-2.2 dB of libjpeg with the
  same tables; the tables are not changed for it.
- The ISP's macroblock stride field has 10 bits, but the A20 uses only
  8: a stride of 4096 makes every macroblock row read the same input.
  The stride limit becomes 4080 for both A20 encoders, which also
  bounds the JPEG width (heights up to 4096 work).

The stream register sequence is the Allwinner library's. EOI is stored
at DQBUF, not in the interrupt handler, as CPU access to an imported
buffer may sleep.

Compared with libjpeg-turbo (ISLOW) at the same tables, the scans are
the entropy coding libjpeg produces for the same coefficients, except
that the VE pads the last byte with 0-bits; PSNR is within 0.12 dB at
qualities up to 90, from 16x16 to 4080x4096 and at odd sizes. A
1920x1080 frame takes 25 ms at quality 75.

Tested on a Cubietruck (A20) and a Rikomagic MK802 (A10).

